    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "root = [4, 2, 6, 1, 3]                  ->  1\nroot = [1, 0, 48, null, null, 12, 49]   ->  1    (48 and 49)\nroot = [10, 5, 20]                      ->  5"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If the values were in a sorted list, the closest pair would be **adjacent** — any value between two others is closer to each of them than they are to each other. A BST hands you that sorted list for free: its **in-order** walk. So walk in-order and compare each value with the one just before it."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Compare every pair"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function getMinimumDifference(root) {\n  const values = [];\n  const walk = (node) => {\n    if (node === null) return;\n    values.push(node.val);\n    walk(node.left);\n    walk(node.right);\n  };\n  walk(root);\n  let best = Infinity;\n  for (let i = 0; i < values.length; i++)\n    for (let j = i + 1; j < values.length; j++) best = Math.min(best, Math.abs(values[i] - values[j]));\n  return best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def get_minimum_difference(root):\n    values = []\n\n    def walk(node):\n        if node is None:\n            return\n        values.append(node.val)\n        walk(node.left)\n        walk(node.right)\n\n    walk(root)\n    best = float(\"inf\")\n    for i in range(len(values)):\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(values)):\n            best = min(best, abs(values[i] - values[j]))\n    return best"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int getMinimumDifference(TreeNode root) {\n    List<Integer> values = new ArrayList<>();\n    walk(root, values);\n    int best = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n    for (int i = 0; i < values.size(); i++)\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < values.size(); j++)\n            best = Math.min(best, Math.abs(values.get(i) - values.get(j)));\n    return best;\n}\n\nvoid walk(TreeNode node, List<Integer> values) {\n    if (node == null) return;\n    values.add(node.val);\n    walk(node.left, values);\n    walk(node.right, values);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "void walk(TreeNode* node, vector<int>& values) {\n    if (node == nullptr) return;\n    values.push_back(node->val);\n    walk(node->left, values);\n    walk(node->right, values);\n}\n\nint getMinimumDifference(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> values;\n    walk(root, values);\n    int best = INT_MAX;\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < values.size(); i++)\n        for (size_t j = i + 1; j < values.size(); j++)\n            best = min(best, abs(values[i] - values[j]));\n    return best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n²), space O(n).** It works on any binary tree — it ignores the ordering completely."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. In-order, compare neighbours"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "min-diff-bst",
      "caption": "In-order visits values in sorted order; only the gap to the previous value can be the new best."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function getMinimumDifference(root) {\n  let prev = null;\n  let best = Infinity;\n  const inorder = (node) => {\n    if (node === null) return;\n    inorder(node.left);\n    if (prev !== null) best = Math.min(best, node.val - prev);   // sorted, so the gap is never negative\n    prev = node.val;\n    inorder(node.right);\n  };\n  inorder(root);\n  return best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def get_minimum_difference(root):\n    prev = None\n    best = float(\"inf\")\n\n    def inorder(node):\n        nonlocal prev, best\n        if node is None:\n            return\n        inorder(node.left)\n        if prev is not None:\n            best = min(best, node.val - prev)   # sorted, so the gap is never negative\n        prev = node.val\n        inorder(node.right)\n\n    inorder(root)\n    return best"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "Integer prev;\nint best;\n\nint getMinimumDifference(TreeNode root) {\n    prev = null;\n    best = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n    inorder(root);\n    return best;\n}\n\nvoid inorder(TreeNode node) {\n    if (node == null) return;\n    inorder(node.left);\n    if (prev != null) best = Math.min(best, node.val - prev);   // sorted, so the gap is never negative\n    prev = node.val;\n    inorder(node.right);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "void inorder(TreeNode* node, int& prev, bool& seen, int& best) {\n    if (node == nullptr) return;\n    inorder(node->left, prev, seen, best);\n    if (seen) best = min(best, node->val - prev);   // sorted, so the gap is never negative\n    prev = node->val;\n    seen = true;\n    inorder(node->right, prev, seen, best);\n}\n\nint getMinimumDifference(TreeNode* root) {\n    int prev = 0, best = INT_MAX;\n    bool seen = false;\n    inorder(root, prev, seen, best);\n    return best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(h).** Same answer as LeetCode 783 (Minimum Distance Between BST Nodes) — it's the same problem."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Compare every pair",
          "time": "O(n²)",
          "timeWhy": "Every pair of the **n** values: about **n²/2** comparisons.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The **list of all values**."
        },
        {
          "approach": "In-order, compare neighbours",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "In-order gives **sorted order**, so only adjacent values need comparing: one visit per node.",
          "space": "O(h)",
          "spaceWhy": "The recursion, plus one **previous value**."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Two nodes** — the answer is their difference.",
        "**The closest pair is far apart in the tree** — e.g. a root and the leftmost node of its right subtree.",
        "**A chain** — h = n, so the recursion is n deep."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Minimum Distance Between BST Nodes** — the identical problem.",
        "**Closest pair in an unsorted array** — sort first, then the same neighbour scan.",
        "**Maximum difference** — no scan needed: max minus min, the two ends of the in-order walk."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-19T08:09:51.478Z"
